Reader bands:

6
00:00:13.396 --> 00:00:15.679
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:20.588
Ch 01, Ch 02, Ch 31, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:40.559
The architect's primary output, a design document that gets the right review, plus the machinery that keeps architecture documentation true: a design-doc linter, C4 diagrams generated from one model, an estimation consistency check, and a drift detector that compares the documented architecture with the code.

9
00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:46.065
This chapter contains the reusable design-document template used throughout the handbook.

10
00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:49.293
L1: design-doc linter.

11
00:00:49.573 --> 00:01:05.039
A plausible-looking one-page doc produced 18 findings, 7 missing sections, 9 adjectives posing as requirements (fast, highly available, real-time), no compared alternatives, a two-word rollout, no decision record.

12
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:06.770
The complete doc passed.

13
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:07.861
L2:

14
00:01:08.141 --> 00:01:09.549
C4 from one model.

15
00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:26.938
One model of 13 elements and 11 relationships generated both the System Context and Container diagrams, and validation caught 3 problems a picture can't show: a container with no owning team, an orphaned search index, and an arrow with no protocol.

16
00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.549
L3: estimation consistency.

17
00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:54.509
Recomputing a doc's capacity section from its own assumptions found 2 of 5 figures off by 2x: peak writes overstated 8.6x (500 per second claimed versus 58 per second derived) and storage understated 2.2x (2.0 TB versus 4.5 TB), the difference between must shard and one PostgreSQL primary.

18
00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:56.411
L4: drift detection.

19
00:01:56.691 --> 00:02:10.365
Scanning real source files recovered 8 actual dependencies; against the 6 drawn in the doc, 3 were undocumented (including a synchronous checkout, then promotions call on the critical path) and 1 was stale.

20
00:02:10.645 --> 00:02:11.627
Reading time:

21
00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:13.528
100 min + code.

22
00:02:14.878 --> 00:02:15.731
Part 1.

23
00:02:16.011 --> 00:02:17.931
Introduction and Why It Matters.

24
00:02:19.181 --> 00:02:23.107
A story: the design that everyone approved and nobody understood.

25
00:02:23.387 --> 00:02:27.333
The platform needed a new slot-booking system for scheduled deliveries.

26
00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:52.381
A senior engineer wrote a 28-page design document: a history of the old system, a dozen screenshots of the current UI, a complete REST API specification, class diagrams for every entity, and, on page 23, two paragraphs describing the actual architecture: slot inventory will be held in Redis for fast access; bookings are written to PostgreSQL asynchronously.

27
00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:54.304
The document went to review.

28
00:02:54.584 --> 00:02:56.291
Eleven people were invited.

29
00:02:56.571 --> 00:02:57.723
Four opened it.

30
00:02:58.003 --> 00:03:00.797
Two left comments, both about API naming.

31
00:03:01.077 --> 00:03:06.347
It was approved in a meeting where the author presented slides that didn't match the document.

32
00:03:07.697 --> 00:03:12.262
Three months after launch, a Redis failover lost thirty seconds of slot bookings.

33
00:03:12.542 --> 00:03:16.105
Customers were promised slots that were then given to someone else.

34
00:03:16.385 --> 00:03:22.145
The postmortem asked: was the risk of losing Redis-held bookings discussed in the design review?

35
00:03:22.425 --> 00:03:23.491
It had not been.

36
00:03:23.771 --> 00:03:32.113
Nobody had noticed that written to PostgreSQL asynchronously meant the source of truth for a customer-visible promise lived in a cache.

37
00:03:32.393 --> 00:03:40.350
The one decision that mattered was buried on page 23, between an ER diagram and a table of HTTP status codes.

38
00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:45.067
The review found that the document failed at communication, not design effort:

39
00:03:46.417 --> 00:03:48.892
No problem statement or goals up front.

40
00:03:49.172 --> 00:03:54.569
Reviewers couldn't tell what the design was optimizing for, so they couldn't judge whether it succeeded.

41
00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:56.556
Requirements were adjectives.

42
00:03:56.836 --> 00:04:01.913
Fast, reliable, scalable, nothing a reviewer could check a design against (L1).

43
00:04:02.193 --> 00:04:04.775
The key decision wasn't framed as a decision.

44
00:04:05.055 --> 00:04:08.340
No alternatives, no trade-offs, no here's what we're risking.

45
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:13.164
The durability trade-off was never presented as a choice (Ch 31).

46
00:04:13.444 --> 00:04:14.831
Wrong level of detail.

47
00:04:15.111 --> 00:04:22.748
Pages of API minutiae (reviewable later, in code) and two paragraphs of architecture (only reviewable now).

48
00:04:24.098 --> 00:04:26.594
Diagrams didn't match each other or the text.

49
00:04:26.874 --> 00:04:31.354
The slide deck and the document showed different components (L2, L4).

50
00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:46.759
The rewrite of the design process, the template in §2.3, the review practices in §2.6, and the tooling in the labs, was aimed at one outcome: the decisions that are expensive to change get discussed before they are made.

51
00:04:48.109 --> 00:04:48.792
Quote:

52
00:04:49.072 --> 00:04:50.736
The thesis of this chapter.

53
00:04:51.016 --> 00:04:59.592
A design document is not a record of work; it is a tool for getting the right feedback on the decisions that are expensive to change, before you make them.

54
00:04:59.872 --> 00:05:19.520
That means: lead with the problem, goals and measurable requirements; present the key decisions as decisions, with alternatives and trade-offs; pitch the detail at the level being reviewed; draw diagrams from a model so they can't contradict each other; and keep the documentation tied to the running system so it stays true.

55
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.635
Everything else is decoration.

56
00:05:22.985 --> 00:05:25.289
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

57
00:05:25.569 --> 00:05:26.913
Here is a quick table:

