96
00:06:39.212 --> 00:06:41.089
Lab: §2.9.

97
00:06:42.439 --> 00:06:43.762
Learning objectives.

98
00:06:44.042 --> 00:06:45.941
By the end you will be able to:

99
00:06:46.221 --> 00:06:52.727
Write a design document with the template in §2.3 that leads reviewers to the decisions that matter.

100
00:06:53.007 --> 00:06:57.103
Pitch detail at the right level for the audience and stage of the design.

101
00:06:57.383 --> 00:07:04.530
Draw architecture with the C4 model and generate diagrams from one model rather than drawing them by hand (L2).

102
00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.885
Make every number in a doc derivable from stated assumptions (L3).

103
00:07:10.235 --> 00:07:16.443
Run an effective design review, asynchronous-first, focused on decisions, with explicit outcomes.

104
00:07:16.723 --> 00:07:22.376
Keep architecture documentation true with linting (L1) and drift detection (L4).

105
00:07:22.656 --> 00:07:28.565
Communicate architecture to different audiences, executives, product, engineers, operators.

106
00:07:29.915 --> 00:07:30.790
Part 2.

107
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.947
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

108
00:07:34.197 --> 00:07:36.331
Intuition: why write it down at all.

109
00:07:36.611 --> 00:07:39.107
Writing a design before building it feels slow.

110
00:07:39.387 --> 00:07:44.037
It's the opposite: a design document is the cheapest place to find a mistake.

111
00:07:44.317 --> 00:07:48.968
Changing a sentence costs minutes; changing a deployed data model costs weeks.

112
00:07:49.248 --> 00:07:52.299
The document also does things a conversation can't:

113
00:07:52.579 --> 00:07:53.859
It forces clarity.

114
00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:59.237
Vague ideas survive in conversation because the listener fills in the gaps charitably.

115
00:07:59.517 --> 00:08:08.051
Written down, we'll cache it has to become we'll cache it where, with what TTL, and what happens on a miss during a failover, and the gaps show.

116
00:08:09.401 --> 00:08:10.617
It scales review.

117
00:08:10.897 --> 00:08:13.862
A meeting reaches the people in the room at that hour.

118
00:08:14.142 --> 00:08:22.803
A document reaches everyone affected, asynchronously, including the team in another time zone who owns the dependency you're about to hammer.

119
00:08:23.083 --> 00:08:24.427
It outlives the author.

120
00:08:24.707 --> 00:08:33.561
Six months later, the doc (and its ADRs) is how the next engineer learns why, the problem of §1.1 in Ch 31.

121
00:08:33.841 --> 00:08:37.766
The right amount of documentation depends on the cost of being wrong.

122
00:08:38.046 --> 00:08:42.142
A two-way-door change inside one service needs a PR description.

123
00:08:42.422 --> 00:08:47.777
A new service, a new datastore, or a change to a shared contract needs a design doc.

124
00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:51.623
Know your audience and your level of detail.

125
00:08:51.903 --> 00:09:02.057
The most common design-doc failure is the wrong altitude, §1.1's 28 pages of API detail around two paragraphs of architecture.

126
00:09:02.337 --> 00:09:05.239
Match detail to the decisions being reviewed now:

127
00:09:06.589 --> 00:09:07.933
Here is a quick table:

128
00:09:08.213 --> 00:09:10.965
Stage or audience, and They need, and Leave out.

129
00:09:11.245 --> 00:09:13.207
Executives, product leadership.

130
00:09:13.487 --> 00:09:14.362
They need:

131
00:09:14.642 --> 00:09:19.207
Problem, business impact, options at a high level, cost, risk, timeline.

132
00:09:19.487 --> 00:09:20.341
Leave out:

133
00:09:20.621 --> 00:09:23.245
Component internals, APIs, schemas.

134
00:09:23.525 --> 00:09:25.573
Architecture review or peer teams.

135
00:09:25.853 --> 00:09:26.727
They need:

136
00:09:27.007 --> 00:09:34.709
Goals, NFRs, key decisions + alternatives + trade-offs, container-level design, dependencies, failure modes.

137
00:09:34.989 --> 00:09:35.842
Leave out:

138
00:09:36.122 --> 00:09:39.130
Class diagrams, full API specs, UI.

139
00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:40.583
Implementing team.

140
00:09:40.863 --> 00:09:41.738
They need:

141
00:09:42.018 --> 00:09:46.242
Component design, data model, API contracts, sequence flows.

142
00:09:46.522 --> 00:09:47.375
Leave out:

143
00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:50.066
Business justification already agreed.

144
00:09:50.346 --> 00:09:51.754
Operators or on-call.

145
00:09:52.034 --> 00:09:52.909
They need:

146
00:09:53.189 --> 00:09:57.349
Deployment, dependencies, SLOs, alerts, runbooks, failure behavior.

147
00:09:57.629 --> 00:09:58.482
Leave out:

148
00:09:58.762 --> 00:10:01.023
Design history, rejected options.

149
00:10:02.373 --> 00:10:13.061
One document can serve several audiences if it's layered: an executive summary a reader can stop after, then the architecture-level design, then appendices with detail.

150
00:10:13.341 --> 00:10:16.712
What it must never do is make the reader dig for the decision.

151
00:10:16.992 --> 00:10:18.315
The inverted pyramid.

152
00:10:18.595 --> 00:10:22.947
Journalists put the most important information first because readers stop reading.

153
00:10:23.227 --> 00:10:34.256
Design docs should too: title, then one-paragraph summary (problem, proposal, key trade-off), then goals and requirements, then the design and its decisions, then everything else.

154
00:10:35.606 --> 00:10:37.291
The design document template.

155
00:10:37.571 --> 00:10:43.438
This is the template used throughout the handbook (the orientation chapter's Ch 32 template).

156
00:10:43.718 --> 00:10:47.153
Sections marked (required) are what L1's linter checks.

157
00:10:48.503 --> 00:10:50.081
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

158
00:10:50.361 --> 00:10:54.137
It runs to 65 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

159
00:10:54.417 --> 00:10:55.953
Design: name Status:

160
00:10:56.233 --> 00:10:58.303
Draft In review Approved Superseded.

161
00:10:58.583 --> 00:11:01.271
Summary (required, at most 1 paragraph).

162
00:11:01.551 --> 00:11:03.641
Context or problem (required).

163
00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:10.395
A few rules make the template work rather than become a form to fill in:

164
00:11:10.675 --> 00:11:20.126
Delete sections that genuinely don't apply, but say why in one line (Cost: negligible, uses existing cluster) so reviewers know it was considered.

165
00:11:20.406 --> 00:11:23.755
Every NFR has a number (L1 flags adjectives).

166
00:11:24.035 --> 00:11:27.833
If you don't know the number, that's an open question, not an adjective.

167
00:11:28.113 --> 00:11:31.334
Every number is derivable from the assumptions (L3).

168
00:11:31.614 --> 00:11:37.331
Every key decision has alternatives, one option presented alone is a rationalization.

169
00:11:38.681 --> 00:11:40.452
Length: as short as possible.

170
00:11:40.732 --> 00:11:43.975
For most designs, 4 to 10 pages plus appendices.

171
00:11:44.255 --> 00:11:50.740
Amazon's six-page narrative format and Google's design docs both favor dense prose over slides.

172
00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:54.117
Diagrams: the C4 model.

173
00:11:54.397 --> 00:12:07.111
Most architecture diagrams are ambiguous: boxes mean services in one place, classes in another, deployment nodes in a third; arrows mean calls, depends on, or data flows without saying which.

174
00:12:07.391 --> 00:12:11.914
Simon Brown's C4 model fixes this with four levels of zoom, like a map:

175
00:12:13.264 --> 00:12:14.608
Here is a quick table:

176
00:12:14.888 --> 00:12:18.344
Level, and Shows, and Audience, and In the grocery platform.

177
00:12:18.624 --> 00:12:19.264
1.

178
00:12:19.544 --> 00:12:20.803
System Context.

179
00:12:21.083 --> 00:12:21.872
Shows:

180
00:12:22.152 --> 00:12:26.909
Your system as one box, its users and the external systems it depends on.

181
00:12:27.189 --> 00:12:28.043
Audience:

182
00:12:28.323 --> 00:12:30.307
Everyone, including non-technical.

183
00:12:30.587 --> 00:12:31.995
In the grocery platform:

184
00:12:32.275 --> 00:12:36.029
Customer, rider, the platform, Razorpay, SMS gateway.

185
00:12:36.309 --> 00:12:37.013
2.

186
00:12:37.293 --> 00:12:38.147
Container.

187
00:12:38.427 --> 00:12:39.216
Shows:

188
00:12:39.496 --> 00:12:43.933
The deployable or runnable units inside your system and how they communicate.

189
00:12:44.213 --> 00:12:45.067
Audience:

190
00:12:45.347 --> 00:12:47.267
Engineers, architects, ops.

191
00:12:47.547 --> 00:12:48.955
In the grocery platform:

192
00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:52.264
App, gateway, checkout, catalog, DBs, Kafka.

193
00:12:52.544 --> 00:12:53.291
3.

194
00:12:53.571 --> 00:12:54.467
Component.

195
00:12:54.747 --> 00:12:55.536
Shows:

196
00:12:55.816 --> 00:12:58.653
The major building blocks inside one container.

197
00:12:58.933 --> 00:12:59.787
Audience:

198
00:13:00.067 --> 00:13:01.773
The team building that container.

199
00:13:02.053 --> 00:13:03.461
In the grocery platform:

200
00:13:03.741 --> 00:13:07.347
Checkout's controllers, reservation logic, payment adapter.

201
00:13:07.627 --> 00:13:08.331
4.

202
00:13:08.611 --> 00:13:09.357
Code.

203
00:13:09.637 --> 00:13:10.427
Shows:

204
00:13:10.707 --> 00:13:11.965
Classes, tables.

205
00:13:12.245 --> 00:13:13.099
Audience:

206
00:13:13.379 --> 00:13:16.515
Rarely drawn by hand, generate from code if needed.

207
00:13:17.865 --> 00:13:29.769
Plus supplementary views: deployment (containers mapped onto infrastructure), dynamic (a sequence of interactions for one scenario), and system landscape (many systems in an enterprise).

208
00:13:30.049 --> 00:13:33.654
C4's discipline is in the notation rules, not the shapes:

209
00:13:33.934 --> 00:13:38.926
Every element has a name, a type, a technology, and a one-line responsibility.

210
00:13:39.206 --> 00:13:45.499
Every arrow is one-directional and labeled with what it does and how (reads prices from, gRPC).

211
00:13:46.849 --> 00:13:54.103
Every diagram has a title and a key, and stays at one level of abstraction, no classes on a container diagram.

212
00:13:54.383 --> 00:14:00.527
Most designs need only Levels 1 and 2, plus a couple of dynamic (sequence) views for key flows.

213
00:14:00.807 --> 00:14:03.516
Level 3 only for complex containers.

214
00:14:03.796 --> 00:14:13.865
This handbook draws C4 diagrams as Mermaid flowcharts with the level named in the title (§3.1 to 3.2 were generated by L2).

215
00:14:15.215 --> 00:14:17.583
Diagrams as code and the single model.

216
00:14:17.863 --> 00:14:29.639
Hand-drawn diagrams drift: the context diagram, the container diagram, the slide deck and the wiki page each get edited independently until they disagree (§1.1).

217
00:14:29.919 --> 00:14:31.455
Two escalating remedies:

218
00:14:31.735 --> 00:14:41.634
Diagrams as text (Mermaid, PlantUML, D2): diagrams live in the repo, are diffable, and are reviewed in PRs alongside the code they describe.

219
00:14:41.914 --> 00:14:43.237
This is the minimum.

220
00:14:44.587 --> 00:14:57.600
Models as code (Structurizr DSL, C4-PlantUML with a shared model, or L2's approach): you define the architecture once, elements and relationships, and generate every view from it.

221
00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:09.763
A rename happens in one place; views can't contradict each other; and the model can be validated (L2 caught a container with no owner, an orphaned element, and an arrow with no protocol).

222
00:15:10.043 --> 00:15:17.552
The model also becomes queryable: what depends on the orders database? is a query rather than an archaeology project.

223
00:15:18.902 --> 00:15:29.590
Design reviews that work. §1.1's review failed in a very common way: a large invite list, a long document, a meeting, and comments about naming.

224
00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.003
Practices that produce better reviews:

225
00:15:32.283 --> 00:15:33.521
Asynchronous first.

226
00:15:33.801 --> 00:15:38.963
Circulate the doc with a deadline (typically 3 to 5 working days) for written comments.

227
00:15:39.243 --> 00:15:44.662
Meetings are for resolving disagreements the comments surfaced, not for reading the doc together.

228
00:15:44.942 --> 00:15:53.774
(Amazon starts meetings with silent reading of a six-pager precisely because pre-reading doesn't reliably happen; either works if it's deliberate.).

229
00:15:55.124 --> 00:15:57.556
Name the reviewers and what you want from each.

230
00:15:57.836 --> 00:16:02.572
Payments team: please check the idempotency design in §5.3.

231
00:16:02.852 --> 00:16:05.625
SRE: failure modes in §7.

232
00:16:05.905 --> 00:16:10.257
A doc sent to eleven people with no ask gets zero useful reviews.

233
00:16:10.537 --> 00:16:12.841
Tell reviewers which decisions are open.

234
00:16:13.121 --> 00:16:20.076
Point them at the key decisions and alternatives; say which parts are settled (don't relitigate) and which you're unsure about.

235
00:16:20.356 --> 00:16:22.148
Lint before humans (L1).

236
00:16:22.428 --> 00:16:28.657
Missing sections and adjective-requirements are the author's job to fix before anyone else spends time.

237
00:16:30.007 --> 00:16:31.351
Explicit outcomes.

238
00:16:31.631 --> 00:16:41.018
Every review ends in one of: approved, approved with changes (listed), needs another round, or rejected, recorded in the doc's status and history.

239
00:16:41.298 --> 00:16:43.410
Comments are resolved, not ignored.

240
00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:50.389
Each substantive comment gets a response: changed, won't change (with reason), or moved to open questions.

241
00:16:50.669 --> 00:17:03.618
Separate facts from preferences (Ch 31 §2.3): this breaches the RPO is a blocking fact; I'd have used Kafka is a preference unless it comes with a reason tied to the requirements.

242
00:17:04.968 --> 00:17:07.763
Reviewer checklist (useful for anyone reviewing):

243
00:17:08.043 --> 00:17:11.776
Can I state the problem and the proposal after reading the summary?

244
00:17:12.056 --> 00:17:14.936
Are the goals measurable, and would the design meet them?

245
00:17:15.216 --> 00:17:16.496
(Can I check a number?).

246
00:17:16.776 --> 00:17:19.016
What happens when each dependency fails?

247
00:17:19.296 --> 00:17:21.152
Where's the single point of failure?

248
00:17:21.432 --> 00:17:25.997
Where does the source of truth for each piece of data live, and what's its durability?

249
00:17:26.277 --> 00:17:28.197
(§1.1's question.).

250
00:17:29.547 --> 00:17:34.689
What's the hardest part to change later, and was it presented as a decision with alternatives?

251
00:17:34.969 --> 00:17:36.526
Is the rollout reversible?

252
00:17:36.806 --> 00:17:38.193
What's the rollback trigger?

253
00:17:38.473 --> 00:17:41.438
Is anything missing that my team will have to deal with?

254
00:17:42.788 --> 00:17:45.369
RFCs, one-pagers and other formats.

255
00:17:45.649 --> 00:17:47.953
Not every proposal is a design doc:

256
00:17:48.233 --> 00:17:55.764
One-pager or pitch, before a design doc: the problem, why now, rough approach, and the ask (two engineers for six weeks).

257
00:17:56.044 --> 00:17:58.945
Used to decide whether to invest in a full design.

258
00:17:59.225 --> 00:18:10.340
RFC (Request for Comments), for cross-cutting proposals that affect many teams (a new API standard, adopting a new datastore, changing the deployment platform).

259
00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:15.271
Open to all engineers with a time-boxed comment period and a named decider.

260
00:18:15.551 --> 00:18:21.183
Rust, Python (PEPs) and many companies (Uber, Sourcegraph, HashiCorp) use variants.

261
00:18:22.533 --> 00:18:25.263
ADR, a single decision (Ch 31).

262
00:18:25.543 --> 00:18:28.594
Design docs link ADRs for their key decisions.

263
00:18:28.874 --> 00:18:47.327
Architecture overview or arc42, for documenting an existing system: arc42 is a 12-section template (goals, constraints, context, solution strategy, building blocks, runtime, deployment, cross-cutting concepts, decisions, quality, risks, glossary) that pairs well with C4.

264
00:18:47.607 --> 00:18:57.058
Postmortem, the design doc written after reality disagreed with the design (Ch 27); its action items often spawn new ADRs.

265
00:18:58.408 --> 00:18:59.859
Numbers in documents.

266
00:19:00.139 --> 00:19:10.016
Design docs are full of numbers, capacity, latency budgets, costs, and those numbers drive decisions: instance counts, database choice, whether to shard.

267
00:19:10.296 --> 00:19:12.536
Yet reviewers rarely recompute them.

268
00:19:12.816 --> 00:19:26.725
L3 shows the typical failure: a peak write rate overstated 8.6x in one line (the reads, computed separately, used the right number), and a storage figure with no working that was 2.2x low.

269
00:19:27.005 --> 00:19:28.264
Rules for numbers:

270
00:19:28.544 --> 00:19:30.656
Separate assumptions from derivations.

271
00:19:30.936 --> 00:19:38.680
State assumptions once (DAU, orders per user, payload size, peak factor, retention); derive everything else from them.

272
00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:42.782
Show the working, a formula a reviewer can check.

273
00:19:43.062 --> 00:19:47.307
A number without derivation can't be challenged except by redoing it.

274
00:19:47.587 --> 00:19:55.118
Generate derived figures where you can (a small script or spreadsheet in the appendix), so the doc's numbers can't drift apart.

275
00:19:55.398 --> 00:20:05.681
Round honestly and state precision, ~58 writes per second not 57.87; ranges where uncertain (Ch 31 L4).

276
00:20:05.961 --> 00:20:08.777
Let reviewers argue with assumptions, not results.

277
00:20:09.057 --> 00:20:15.094
I think 0.5 orders per DAU is too high for weekdays is a productive review comment.

278
00:20:16.444 --> 00:20:18.897
Living documentation: keeping it true.

279
00:20:19.177 --> 00:20:23.188
Documentation rots because the system changes and the docs don't.

280
00:20:23.468 --> 00:20:25.793
Strategies, from weakest to strongest:

281
00:20:26.073 --> 00:20:33.135
Docs next to code, changed in the same PR (docs-as-code): the reviewer sees the diagram diff with the code diff.

282
00:20:33.415 --> 00:20:37.297
Status and ownership on every doc, with a last verified date.

283
00:20:37.577 --> 00:20:40.479
Stale docs get marked, not silently trusted.

284
00:20:40.759 --> 00:20:42.636
Generate what can be generated:

285
00:20:42.916 --> 00:20:55.417
API references from OpenAPI or AsyncAPI specs, schema docs from migrations, C4 container views from the model (L2), dependency views from runtime telemetry.

286
00:20:56.767 --> 00:21:10.847
Detect drift automatically (L4): recover the actual architecture, from code, from service-mesh telemetry, or from distributed traces (Ch 24), and diff it against the documented model in CI.

287
00:21:11.127 --> 00:21:19.789
The undocumented dependencies are usually the dangerous ones (L4 found an unreviewed synchronous call on the checkout critical path).

288
00:21:20.069 --> 00:21:30.309
Make documentation executable: fitness functions (Ch 31 L2) are architecture documentation that fails the build when it stops being true.

289
00:21:31.659 --> 00:21:41.941
The design doc itself is a point-in-time artifact: once approved and built, mark it as such and let the model, ADRs and generated docs carry the current truth.

290
00:21:42.221 --> 00:21:48.216
Don't try to keep every design doc eternally up to date; keep the living artifacts true and link to them.

291
00:21:49.566 --> 00:21:52.254
Communicating architecture beyond documents.

292
00:21:52.534 --> 00:21:56.523
Senior architects spend as much time communicating as designing:

293
00:21:56.803 --> 00:22:03.481
For executives: lead with business outcome, risk and cost; one diagram (context level); the ask.

294
00:22:03.761 --> 00:22:12.358
Frame technical options as business options (ship in 6 weeks with a known scaling limit at 3x today's load, or 12 weeks without it).

295
00:22:12.638 --> 00:22:19.251
For product: what users will experience, what's possible now versus later, what the constraints are and why.

296
00:22:19.531 --> 00:22:25.099
For engineers across teams: contracts, dependencies, timelines, and what you need from them.

297
00:22:26.449 --> 00:22:31.292
For operators: failure modes, SLOs, runbooks, and what's changing in their pager.

298
00:22:31.572 --> 00:22:38.889
Storytelling with evidence: a before or after with numbers (latency, incidents, cost) beats any architecture slide.

299
00:22:39.169 --> 00:22:47.233
Architecture katas and brown-bags spread design skill; architecture decision logs made visible to everyone spread context.

300
00:22:47.513 --> 00:22:52.676
Say I don't know in writing, the open questions section is a sign of rigor, not weakness.

301
00:22:54.026 --> 00:22:54.922
Part 3.

302
00:22:55.202 --> 00:22:56.930
Architecture and Diagrams.

