00:35:51.337 --> 00:35:52.851
The rest is in the chapter.

448
00:35:55.701 --> 00:35:59.285
L3, Are the doc's numbers consistent with its own assumptions?

449
00:35:59.565 --> 00:36:00.504
Requirement:

450
00:36:00.784 --> 00:36:05.029
The capacity section says 500 orders per second and 2 TB.

451
00:36:05.309 --> 00:36:09.384
Before we size the cluster, check those against the doc's stated assumptions.

452
00:36:09.664 --> 00:36:19.883
Every derived figure is recomputed from the doc's assumptions with the Ch 02 method and compared with the claim; anything off by more than 2x is flagged.

453
00:36:21.233 --> 00:36:24.198
Here is the Java code, EstimateL3.java.

454
00:36:24.478 --> 00:36:28.254
It runs to 97 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

455
00:36:28.534 --> 00:36:35.873
EstimateL3.java, run with: java EstimateL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

456
00:36:36.153 --> 00:36:39.353
INTERNAL CONSISTENCY OF A DESIGN DOC'S NUMBERS.

457
00:36:39.633 --> 00:36:52.369
Design docs state assumptions (DAU, orders per user, payload size. retention) in one section and derived figures (peak QPS, storage, bandwidth) in another, often written on different.

458
00:36:55.219 --> 00:36:56.136
On screen:

459
00:36:56.416 --> 00:37:03.157
Recomputing a design doc's capacity figures from its own stated assumptions (flag if. off by 2x).

460
00:37:03.437 --> 00:37:04.376
Assumptions:

461
00:37:04.656 --> 00:37:12.379
2,000,000 DAU, 0.5 orders or DAU per day, peak times 5, 4 KiB per order, 3 yr. retention,

462
00:37:12.659 --> 00:37:44.610
40 reads per order. figure claimed derived ratio derivation. avg order writes per second 11.6 per second 11.6 per second 1.0x 2M DAU times 0.5 ÷ 86,400. peak order writes per second 500 per second 57.9 per second 8.6x avg times peak factor 5 INCONSISTENT. peak order reads per second 2,300 per

463
00:37:44.890 --> 00:38:04.303
second 2,315 per second 1.0x peak writes times 40 reads per order. order storage (3 yr, 1 copy) 2.0 TB 4.5 TB 0.4x 1M per day times 365 times 3 times 4 KiB.

464
00:38:04.583 --> 00:38:05.671
INCONSISTENT.

465
00:38:05.951 --> 00:38:16.895
Kafka ingest (3 replicas)/day 4.2 GB 4.6 GB 0.9x 1M per day times 1.5 KiB times 3.

466
00:38:17.175 --> 00:38:18.690
The rest is in the chapter.

467
00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:22.393
Reading it:

468
00:38:22.673 --> 00:38:23.633
Peak writes:

469
00:38:23.913 --> 00:38:30.036
500 per second claimed versus 58 per second derived (8.6x over).

470
00:38:30.316 --> 00:38:47.660
The peak reads figure (2,300 per second) matches the correct derivation (58 times 40 = 2,315), so the author clearly had the right write rate when computing reads, the 500 is an isolated slip that nobody recomputed.

471
00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:48.836
Storage:

472
00:38:49.116 --> 00:39:00.956
2.0 TB claimed versus 4.5 TB derived (2.2x under), with no working shown, so a reviewer can't even tell which assumption was silently changed.

473
00:39:02.306 --> 00:39:03.991
Both errors change decisions:

474
00:39:04.271 --> 00:39:13.338
500 writes per second pushes toward sharding (Ch 11); 58 per second is comfortable on one PostgreSQL primary.

475
00:39:13.618 --> 00:39:17.650
An understated storage figure under-provisions disks and backups.

476
00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:28.319
The fix is structural, not be more careful: put assumptions in one place and generate the derived numbers, so reviewers argue about assumptions and the figures can't drift.

477
00:39:29.669 --> 00:39:32.613
L4, Detecting drift between the doc and the code.

478
00:39:32.893 --> 00:39:33.832
Requirement:

479
00:39:34.112 --> 00:39:37.056
The container diagram was approved six months ago.

480
00:39:37.336 --> 00:39:38.616
What has changed since?

481
00:39:38.896 --> 00:39:56.240
The lab parses a real (small) Java source tree, Feign clients for sync calls, Kafka send/@KafkaListener for async, WebClient.create for external hosts, recovers the actual dependency graph, and diffs it against the relationships drawn in the design doc.

482
00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:03.990
Here is the Java code, src/checkout/CheckoutService.java (one of the scanned files).

483
00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:09.613
Here is the Java code, DriftL4.java.

484
00:40:09.893 --> 00:40:13.669
It runs to 97 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

485
00:40:13.949 --> 00:40:23.699
DriftL4.java, run with: java DriftL4.java src documentedmodel.txt (JDK 21, no dependencies).

486
00:40:23.979 --> 00:40:25.813
DOCUMENTATION DRIFT DETECTION.

487
00:40:26.093 --> 00:40:32.088
A design doc's container diagram is true on the day it's approved and starts rotting the. next day.

488
00:40:32.368 --> 00:40:39.259
Here we recover the ACTUAL architecture from the source code, Feign clients (sync calls), Kafka producers and.

489
00:40:42.109 --> 00:40:43.026
On screen:

490
00:40:43.306 --> 00:40:50.133
Architecture drift: dependencies recovered from source code versus relationships drawn in. the design doc.

491
00:40:50.413 --> 00:41:10.551
Recovered from code (8): catalog - topic:price-changes (catalog or CatalogService.java:4). checkout - catalog (checkout or CheckoutService.java:2). checkout - ext:razorpay (checkout or PaymentAdapter.java:3). checkout - promotions (checkout or CheckoutService.java:4). checkout - topic:order-events

492
00:41:10.831 --> 00:41:27.450
(checkout or CheckoutService.java:8). dispatch - ext:sms (dispatch or DispatchListener.java:4). promotions - catalog (promotions or PromotionsService.java:2). topic:order-events - dispatch (dispatch or DispatchListener.java:3).

493
00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:32.871
Documented in the design doc (6): checkout - catalog, checkout - ext:razorpay.

494
00:41:33.151 --> 00:41:34.666
The rest is in the chapter.

495
00:41:37.516 --> 00:41:38.369
Reading it:

496
00:41:38.649 --> 00:41:46.372
8 real dependencies were recovered with file-and-line evidence; 5 of the 6 documented relationships still hold.

497
00:41:46.652 --> 00:41:48.167
3 are undocumented.

498
00:41:48.447 --> 00:41:56.553
The important one is checkout, then promotions: a synchronous call added to the checkout critical path that no design review saw.

499
00:41:56.833 --> 00:42:10.401
Its latency now sits inside the checkout p99 budget (Ch 31 L3), and a promotions outage now becomes a checkout outage unless someone adds a timeout and fallback (Ch 18).

500
00:42:10.681 --> 00:42:13.775
Promotions itself calls catalog, deepening the chain.

501
00:42:15.125 --> 00:42:27.221
1 is stale: dispatch, then checkout was removed from the code (the scanner ignores the comment mentioning it) but is still on the diagram, where it misleads anyone reasoning about blast radius.

502
00:42:27.501 --> 00:42:41.559
In production, combine sources: code scanning (intent, including rare paths), distributed traces or service-mesh telemetry (reality, including config-driven calls, Ch 24), and infrastructure inventories.

503
00:42:41.839 --> 00:42:49.178
Report drift on every PR and weekly per domain; better still, generate the container view from the recovered graph.

504
00:42:50.528 --> 00:42:51.467
Part 6.

505
00:42:51.747 --> 00:42:53.027
Production Concerns.

506
00:42:54.277 --> 00:42:57.007
Failure modes of architecture documentation.

507
00:42:57.287 --> 00:42:58.631
Here is a quick table:

508
00:42:58.911 --> 00:43:01.194
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

509
00:43:01.474 --> 00:43:02.583
Buried decision.

510
00:43:02.863 --> 00:43:03.674
Symptom:

511
00:43:03.954 --> 00:43:07.709
Key trade-off never discussed in review (§1.1).

512
00:43:07.989 --> 00:43:08.991
Mitigation:

513
00:43:09.271 --> 00:43:12.151
Key decisions section with alternatives up front.

514
00:43:12.431 --> 00:43:13.861
Adjective requirements.

515
00:43:14.141 --> 00:43:14.951
Symptom:

516
00:43:15.231 --> 00:43:17.130
Nothing to check the design against.

517
00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:18.413
Mitigation:

518
00:43:18.693 --> 00:43:21.253
Every NFR has a number; lint (L1).

519
00:43:21.533 --> 00:43:22.685
Wrong altitude.

520
00:43:22.965 --> 00:43:23.775
Symptom:

521
00:43:24.055 --> 00:43:26.722
Reviewers comment on naming, miss architecture.

522
00:43:27.002 --> 00:43:28.005
Mitigation:

523
00:43:28.285 --> 00:43:31.933
Layered doc; detail in appendices (§2.2).

524
00:43:32.213 --> 00:43:33.621
Inconsistent numbers.

525
00:43:33.901 --> 00:43:34.711
Symptom:

526
00:43:34.991 --> 00:43:37.509
Wrong capacity plan, wrong DB choice.

527
00:43:37.789 --> 00:43:38.791
Mitigation:

528
00:43:39.071 --> 00:43:42.399
Assumptions, then derivations, recomputed (L3).

529
00:43:42.679 --> 00:43:44.215
Contradicting diagrams.

530
00:43:44.495 --> 00:43:45.306
Symptom:

531
00:43:45.586 --> 00:43:47.570
Slides, doc, wiki disagree.

532
00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:48.853
Mitigation:

533
00:43:49.133 --> 00:43:51.522
One model, generated views (L2).

534
00:43:52.872 --> 00:43:53.939
Stale docs.

535
00:43:54.219 --> 00:43:55.029
Symptom:

536
00:43:55.309 --> 00:43:58.147
Diagram shows removed deps, misses new ones.

537
00:43:58.427 --> 00:43:59.429
Mitigation:

538
00:43:59.709 --> 00:44:02.845
Drift detection versus code or traces (L4).

539
00:44:03.125 --> 00:44:04.128
Review theatre.

540
00:44:04.408 --> 00:44:05.219
Symptom:

541
00:44:05.499 --> 00:44:08.912
Many invitees, no substantive comments, no outcome.

542
00:44:09.192 --> 00:44:10.195
Mitigation:

543
00:44:10.475 --> 00:44:14.741
Named asks, async window, recorded outcome (§2.6).

544
00:44:15.021 --> 00:44:16.493
Doc as bureaucracy.

545
00:44:16.773 --> 00:44:17.584
Symptom:

546
00:44:17.864 --> 00:44:20.125
Template filled mechanically, no thinking.

547
00:44:20.405 --> 00:44:21.408
Mitigation:

548
00:44:21.688 --> 00:44:27.960
Drop sections with a reason; short docs; reviewers ask the §2.6 checklist questions.

549
00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:31.550
Observability of the documentation.

550
00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:40.577
Track doc lint findings, drift counts and last verified age per domain; a rising drift count is architecture erosion made visible.

551
00:44:40.857 --> 00:44:52.739
Runtime-recovered dependency graphs (from traces, Ch 24) are the most honest drift source, they include dependencies that exist only via configuration or reflection.

552
00:44:53.019 --> 00:45:00.955
Measure review health: time from In review to outcome, number of substantive comments, percentage of reviews with a recorded outcome.

553
00:45:02.305 --> 00:45:03.244
Security.

554
00:45:03.524 --> 00:45:08.921
Design docs often contain threat models, trust boundaries, and secrets-handling details.

555
00:45:09.201 --> 00:45:20.743
Keep sensitive sections in access-controlled repos or appendices; never paste real credentials, internal hostnames or customer data into examples (Ch 23).

556
00:45:21.023 --> 00:45:30.921
The Security and privacy section is required: trust boundaries on the container diagram, data classification per store, authn or authz per interface.

557
00:45:31.201 --> 00:45:36.919
Security reviewers should get a specific ask (§2.6), not a generic invite.

558
00:45:38.269 --> 00:45:39.805
Performance of the process.

559
00:45:40.085 --> 00:45:46.933
Lint and estimation checks run in seconds, make them PR checks so authors get feedback before reviewers do.

560
00:45:47.213 --> 00:45:55.255
Time-box reviews (3 to 5 days); unresolved items become open questions with owners rather than blocking indefinitely.

561
00:45:55.535 --> 00:46:03.962
Reuse: link standard sections (for example, the platform's standard observability setup) rather than rewriting them in every doc.

562
00:46:05.312 --> 00:46:06.037
Cost.

563
00:46:06.317 --> 00:46:17.304
A design doc costs a few days of an engineer's time; §1.1's missed durability decision cost a customer-facing incident, a redesign and a migration.

564
00:46:17.584 --> 00:46:25.648
The return on a well-reviewed design is highest for one-way-door decisions, scale the effort to the reversibility (Ch 31).

565
00:46:26.998 --> 00:46:27.937
Part 7.

566
00:46:28.217 --> 00:46:29.923
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

567
00:46:31.173 --> 00:46:32.091
Watch out:

568
00:46:32.371 --> 00:46:33.011
1.

569
00:46:33.291 --> 00:46:34.485
The buried decision.

570
00:46:34.765 --> 00:46:43.213
The expensive-to-reverse choice sits on page 23 between an ER diagram and a status-code table (§1.1).

571
00:46:43.493 --> 00:46:46.629
Put key decisions in their own section, with alternatives.

572
00:46:47.979 --> 00:46:48.897
Watch out:

573
00:46:49.177 --> 00:46:49.881
2.

574
00:46:50.161 --> 00:46:51.867
Adjectives as requirements.

575
00:46:52.147 --> 00:46:56.094
Fast, scalable, highly available give reviewers nothing to check.

576
00:46:56.374 --> 00:46:58.593
Every NFR gets a number (L1).

577
00:46:59.943 --> 00:47:00.860
Watch out:

578
00:47:01.140 --> 00:47:01.887
3.

579
00:47:02.167 --> 00:47:02.999
The novel.

580
00:47:03.279 --> 00:47:06.009
A 28-page doc that nobody reads fully.

581
00:47:06.289 --> 00:47:10.364
Keep it to what reviewers must decide on; move detail to appendices.

582
00:47:11.714 --> 00:47:12.631
Watch out:

583
00:47:12.911 --> 00:47:13.615
4.

584
00:47:13.895 --> 00:47:15.773
The slide deck instead of a doc.

585
00:47:16.053 --> 00:47:20.234
Slides hide gaps behind a presenter's voice and don't survive the meeting.

586
00:47:20.514 --> 00:47:23.991
Write prose; use slides only to summarize a written doc.

587
00:47:25.341 --> 00:47:26.259
Watch out:

588
00:47:26.539 --> 00:47:27.307
5.

589
00:47:27.587 --> 00:47:29.379
Unlabeled boxes and arrows.

590
00:47:29.659 --> 00:47:34.416
An arrow without direction, verb and protocol means different things to every reader.

591
00:47:34.696 --> 00:47:37.917
Use C4 notation rules (§2.4).

592
00:47:39.267 --> 00:47:40.185
Watch out:

593
00:47:40.465 --> 00:47:41.211
6.

594
00:47:41.491 --> 00:47:43.006
Mixed abstraction levels.

595
00:47:43.286 --> 00:47:47.595
Classes on a container diagram, infrastructure on a context diagram.

596
00:47:47.875 --> 00:47:49.326
One level per diagram.

597
00:47:50.676 --> 00:47:51.593
Watch out:

598
00:47:51.873 --> 00:47:52.620
7.

599
00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:55.183
Hand-maintained duplicate diagrams.

600
00:47:55.463 --> 00:47:59.772
Context, container, slides and wiki drawn separately will disagree.

601
00:48:00.052 --> 00:48:02.441
Generate views from one model (L2).

602
00:48:03.791 --> 00:48:04.709
Watch out:

603
00:48:04.989 --> 00:48:05.671
8.

604
00:48:05.951 --> 00:48:07.445
Numbers with no working.

605
00:48:07.725 --> 00:48:12.013
Reviewers can't challenge them and errors propagate into capacity plans.

606
00:48:12.293 --> 00:48:15.066
Assumptions first, derivations shown (L3).

607
00:48:16.416 --> 00:48:17.333
Watch out:

608
00:48:17.613 --> 00:48:18.360
9.

609
00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:19.771
Review by meeting.

610
00:48:20.051 --> 00:48:25.064
Reading the doc together in a meeting reaches few people and produces shallow feedback.

611
00:48:25.344 --> 00:48:27.648
Async first, meeting for disagreements.

612
00:48:28.998 --> 00:48:29.915
Watch out:

613
00:48:30.195 --> 00:48:30.899
10.

614
00:48:31.179 --> 00:48:32.481
No review outcome.

615
00:48:32.761 --> 00:48:34.723
We discussed it isn't a decision.

616
00:48:35.003 --> 00:48:38.289
Record approved or changes or rejected in the doc.

617
00:48:39.639 --> 00:48:40.556
Watch out:

618
00:48:40.836 --> 00:48:41.668
11.

619
00:48:41.948 --> 00:48:44.956
Treating the design doc as living documentation.

620
00:48:45.236 --> 00:48:48.287
Nobody updates a 10-page design doc for years.

621
00:48:48.567 --> 00:48:53.964
Keep the model, ADRs and generated docs true; mark the design doc point-in-time.

622
00:48:55.314 --> 00:48:56.231
Watch out:

623
00:48:56.511 --> 00:48:57.301
12.

624
00:48:57.581 --> 00:48:58.989
No open questions.

625
00:48:59.269 --> 00:49:02.554
A doc with no unknowns is either trivial or hiding them.

626
00:49:02.834 --> 00:49:04.711
List what you don't know, with owners.

627
00:49:06.061 --> 00:49:06.936
Part 8.

628
00:49:07.216 --> 00:49:08.560
Interview Q and A.

629
00:49:09.810 --> 00:49:10.642
Beginner.

630
00:49:10.922 --> 00:49:11.946
Question 1.

631
00:49:12.226 --> 00:49:14.402
What goes into a good design document?

632
00:49:14.682 --> 00:49:16.047
Thirty-second answer:

633
00:49:16.327 --> 00:49:32.413
A summary; the problem with evidence; goals and non-goals; measurable requirements (NFRs with numbers) and constraints; scale assumptions with derived figures; the proposed design at the right level (context and container diagrams, key flows,

634
00:49:32.693 --> 00:49:43.957
data ownership); the key decisions with alternatives considered; trade-offs, risks and failure modes; security; observability; rollout and rollback; cost; and open questions.

635
00:49:44.237 --> 00:49:46.434
It leads with what the reader must decide on.

636
00:49:47.784 --> 00:49:48.787
Question 2.

637
00:49:49.067 --> 00:49:50.560
What is the C4 model?

638
00:49:50.840 --> 00:49:52.205
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:49:52.485 --> 00:49:55.877
A way to draw software architecture at four levels of zoom:

640
00:49:56.157 --> 00:50:07.656
System Context (your system, its users and external systems), Container (the deployable units and how they talk), Component (the building blocks inside one container), and Code.

641
00:50:07.936 --> 00:50:15.189
Each diagram stays at one level, and every element and arrow is labeled with name, type or technology and purpose.

642
00:50:15.469 --> 00:50:19.544
Most designs need Levels 1 and 2 plus a few sequence diagrams.

643
00:50:20.894 --> 00:50:21.939
Question 3.

644
00:50:22.219 --> 00:50:23.542
Why write non-goals?

645
00:50:23.822 --> 00:50:25.187
Thirty-second answer:

646
00:50:25.467 --> 00:50:27.259
They draw the boundary of the design.

647
00:50:27.539 --> 00:50:42.387
They stop reviewers from spending comments on things you're deliberately not doing, stop scope creep during implementation, and make trade-offs explicit, we are not replacing the payment provider tells everyone the design must work with the existing one.

648
00:50:43.737 --> 00:50:44.804
Question 4.

649
00:50:45.084 --> 00:50:46.919
What makes a requirement 'measurable'?

650
00:50:47.199 --> 00:50:48.564
Thirty-second answer:

651
00:50:48.844 --> 00:51:03.372
It has a number and a condition you can test: checkout p99 300 ms at 800 req per second, 99.95% monthly availability for POST or checkout, RPO 0 for orders.

652
00:51:03.652 --> 00:51:09.092
Fast, reliable and scalable aren't requirements because no design can be checked against them.

653
00:51:10.442 --> 00:51:11.445
Intermediate.

654
00:51:11.725 --> 00:51:12.834
Question 5.

655
00:51:13.114 --> 00:51:16.485
How do you run a design review that produces useful feedback?

656
00:51:16.765 --> 00:51:18.130
Thirty-second answer:

657
00:51:18.410 --> 00:51:21.845
Lint first so reviewers don't waste time on missing sections.

658
00:51:22.125 --> 00:51:24.855
Name reviewers and give each a specific ask.

659
00:51:25.135 --> 00:51:30.725
Run it asynchronously with a deadline for written comments, and point people at the open decisions.

660
00:51:31.005 --> 00:51:34.354
Hold a meeting only for disagreements the comments surfaced.

661
00:51:34.634 --> 00:51:43.893
Resolve every substantive comment explicitly, and end with a recorded outcome: approved, approved with changes, another round, or rejected.

662
00:51:45.243 --> 00:51:46.352
Question 6.

663
00:51:46.632 --> 00:51:49.939
Why generate diagrams from a model instead of drawing them?

664
00:51:50.219 --> 00:51:51.584
Thirty-second answer:

665
00:51:51.864 --> 00:52:00.525
Hand-drawn diagrams drift: context, container, slides and wiki get edited separately and start contradicting each other and the system.

666
00:52:00.805 --> 00:52:13.947
With one model, every view is generated, so they can't disagree, renames happen once, and the model can be validated and queried, which container has no owner, which element is orphaned, which arrows lack a protocol.

667
00:52:15.297 --> 00:52:16.385
Question 7.

668
00:52:16.665 --> 00:52:19.630
How do you make sure the numbers in a design doc are right?

669
00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:21.275
Thirty-second answer:

670
00:52:21.555 --> 00:52:31.155
Separate assumptions from derived figures, show the working for each derivation, and ideally generate the derived figures from the assumptions with a small script.

671
00:52:31.435 --> 00:52:37.558
Then reviewers argue about assumptions (which is productive) and the derived numbers can't drift apart.

672
00:52:38.908 --> 00:52:39.932
Question 8.

673
00:52:40.212 --> 00:52:44.500
Design doc versus ADR versus RFC, when do you use each?

674
00:52:44.780 --> 00:52:46.145
Thirty-second answer:

675
00:52:46.425 --> 00:52:52.335
A design doc describes how to build something non-trivial and gets feedback on its decisions before building.

676
00:52:52.615 --> 00:53:00.017
An ADR records one significant decision and its reasoning, design docs link ADRs for their key decisions.

677
00:53:00.297 --> 00:53:10.537
An RFC proposes a cross-cutting change affecting many teams (a new standard, platform or technology), open to broad comment with a named decider.

678
00:53:11.887 --> 00:53:12.954
Question 9.

679
00:53:13.234 --> 00:53:15.709
What should a design doc say about failure modes?

680
00:53:15.989 --> 00:53:17.354
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:53:17.634 --> 00:53:28.727
For each dependency and each stateful component: what happens when it's slow, down, or returns wrong data; what the user sees; how the system detects it; and how it recovers.

682
00:53:29.007 --> 00:53:35.279
Also where the source of truth for each piece of data lives and its durability (RPO or RTO).

683
00:53:35.559 --> 00:53:38.674
It should name single points of failure and the mitigations.

684
00:53:40.024 --> 00:53:40.792
Senior.

685
00:53:41.072 --> 00:53:42.117
Question 10.

686
00:53:42.397 --> 00:53:45.384
How do you keep architecture documentation from rotting?

687
00:53:45.664 --> 00:53:47.029
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:53:47.309 --> 00:53:55.331
Keep docs next to code and change them in the same PR; generate what can be generated (API references, schema docs,

689
00:53:55.611 --> 00:54:14.384
diagrams from the model); detect drift automatically by recovering the actual architecture from code or traces and diffing it against the documented model; make rules executable as fitness functions; and treat design docs as point-in-time, keeping the living truth in the model and ADRs.

690
00:54:15.734 --> 00:54:16.865
Question 11.

691
00:54:17.145 --> 00:54:21.347
How do you pitch the same architecture to executives and to engineers?

692
00:54:21.627 --> 00:54:22.993
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:54:23.273 --> 00:54:25.150
Same facts, different altitude.

694
00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:35.115
Executives: business problem, options framed as business trade-offs (time, cost, risk, scalability limits), one context diagram, the ask.

695
00:54:35.395 --> 00:54:43.417
Engineers: requirements with numbers, container-level design, key decisions and alternatives, failure modes, contracts, rollout.

696
00:54:43.697 --> 00:54:47.131
Layer the document so both can stop reading at the right point.

697
00:54:48.481 --> 00:54:49.569
Question 12.

698
00:54:49.849 --> 00:54:54.223
What level of detail belongs in a design doc versus in code review?

699
00:54:54.503 --> 00:54:55.868
Thirty-second answer:

700
00:54:56.148 --> 00:55:10.911
The design doc covers decisions that are expensive to change and that cross boundaries: service boundaries, data ownership and durability, integration styles, contracts between teams, consistency model, failure behavior, rollout.

701
00:55:11.191 --> 00:55:20.065
Code review covers what's cheap to change: internal class structure, naming, algorithms within a component, most API field details.

702
00:55:21.415 --> 00:55:22.695
Question 13.

703
00:55:22.975 --> 00:55:27.349
How do you handle a design review where a senior reviewer strongly disagrees?

704
00:55:27.629 --> 00:55:28.994
Thirty-second answer:

705
00:55:29.274 --> 00:55:35.034
Make the disagreement concrete: is it a fact (the design violates a requirement) or a preference?

706
00:55:35.314 --> 00:55:38.365
Facts get evidence, a model, a spike, a benchmark.

707
00:55:38.645 --> 00:55:44.490
Preferences get weighed against the requirements; if they don't change an outcome, record them and move on.

708
00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:54.690
For genuine trade-off disagreements, use a decision matrix with sensitivity (Ch 31) and escalate priority conflicts to the owner of the priority.

709
00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:57.235
Question 14.

710
00:55:57.515 --> 00:56:00.501
What belongs in the rollout section of a design doc?

711
00:56:00.781 --> 00:56:02.147
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:56:02.427 --> 00:56:20.837
The phases (shadow, canary percentages, full), the feature flags, any data migration and its order (expand or contract), the guardrail metrics that gate each phase, the rollback trigger and procedure, including what happens to data written by the new version if you roll back, and who is on point.

713
00:56:22.187 --> 00:56:23.083
Architect.

714
00:56:23.363 --> 00:56:24.558
Question 15.

715
00:56:24.838 --> 00:56:30.043
How would you set up architecture documentation for a 200-engineer organization?

716
00:56:30.323 --> 00:56:31.689
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:56:31.969 --> 00:56:40.331
A small set of formats with clear purposes: one-pagers, design docs (one template), ADRs, RFCs for cross-cutting change.

718
00:56:40.611 --> 00:56:42.723
Everything as code in the owning repos.

719
00:56:43.003 --> 00:56:49.297
A C4 model per domain as the single source of diagrams, rendered in CI into a docs portal.

720
00:56:49.577 --> 00:56:56.510
Automated checks: doc lint, estimation consistency, model validation, drift against code and traces.

721
00:56:56.790 --> 00:57:00.097
Async reviews with named asks and recorded outcomes.

722
00:57:00.377 --> 00:57:05.881
Living truth in model + ADRs + generated docs; design docs point-in-time.

723
00:57:07.231 --> 00:57:08.425
Question 16.

724
00:57:08.705 --> 00:57:12.972
How do you recover the real architecture of a system you just inherited?

725
00:57:13.252 --> 00:57:14.617
Thirty-second answer:

726
00:57:14.897 --> 00:57:31.409
Combine sources: distributed traces and service-mesh telemetry for runtime dependencies; code scanning for clients, topics and datastores; infrastructure-as-code and cloud inventories for deployed resources; database grants for who touches which data.

727
00:57:31.689 --> 00:57:42.079
Merge them into a model, diff against whatever documentation exists, and interview owners about the differences, the undocumented dependencies are where the surprises live.

728
00:57:43.429 --> 00:57:44.773
Question 17.

729
00:57:45.053 --> 00:57:49.170
What are the trade-offs between written design docs and design-by-whiteboard?

730
00:57:49.450 --> 00:57:50.815
Thirty-second answer:

731
00:57:51.095 --> 00:58:02.210
Whiteboarding is fast, collaborative and great for exploring options, but its reasoning evaporates, it reaches only the people present, and gaps get filled charitably by the room.

732
00:58:02.490 --> 00:58:08.271
Written docs are slower but force precision, scale review to everyone affected, and leave a record.

733
00:58:08.551 --> 00:58:13.501
The best practice combines them: explore on a whiteboard, then write the decision down.

734
00:58:14.851 --> 00:58:16.024
Question 18.

735
00:58:16.304 --> 00:58:20.699
How do you use documentation to reduce operational risk after launch?

736
00:58:20.979 --> 00:58:22.344
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:58:22.624 --> 00:58:43.317
The design doc's failure-mode and observability sections become runbooks, alerts and dashboards; SLOs defined in the doc become monitored; the container model tells on-call what depends on what (blast radius); drift detection flags new unreviewed dependencies; and postmortems feed back into ADRs and the model.

738
00:58:43.597 --> 00:58:47.907
Documentation is part of the operational system, not separate from it.

739
00:58:49.257 --> 00:58:50.494
Question 19.

740
00:58:50.774 --> 00:58:54.251
How do you write a design doc when requirements are still uncertain?

741
00:58:54.531 --> 00:58:55.897
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:58:56.177 --> 00:59:12.923
State the uncertainty explicitly: give ranges for assumptions, list open questions with owners and dates, design for the range (not a single point), identify which decisions are one-way doors and defer them if possible, and choose options that keep flexibility.

743
00:59:13.203 --> 00:59:19.923
Where the uncertainty is quantitative, show how the design behaves across the range (Ch 31 L4).

744
00:59:21.273 --> 00:59:22.383
Question 20.

745
00:59:22.663 --> 00:59:27.100
What distinguishes a principal engineer's design doc from a senior engineer's?

746
00:59:27.380 --> 00:59:28.745
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:59:29.025 --> 00:59:30.327
Scope and emphasis.

748
00:59:30.607 --> 00:59:32.847
A senior's doc designs a system well.

749
00:59:33.127 --> 00:59:50.599
A principal's doc frames the problem in business terms, sets measurable goals others can check, presents the few decisions that matter as decisions with alternatives and quantified trade-offs, anticipates cross-team and organizational impact, designs the rollout and the reversibility,

750
00:59:50.879 --> 00:59:55.935
states uncertainty honestly, and is readable by executives and implementers alike.

751
00:59:56.215 --> 00:59:57.409
It's also short.

752
00:59:58.759 --> 00:59:59.655
Part 9.

753
00:59:59.935 --> 01:00:01.343
Summary and Practice.

754
01:00:02.593 --> 01:00:03.681
Key takeaways.

755
01:00:03.961 --> 01:00:11.599
A design doc is a tool for feedback on expensive decisions before they're made, not a record of effort (§1.1).

756
01:00:11.879 --> 01:00:21.649
Lead with the summary, problem, goals and measurable requirements; put the key decisions where reviewers can't miss them (§2.2, §2.3).

757
01:00:21.929 --> 01:00:26.559
Use the template (§2.3) and drop sections only with a reason.

758
01:00:26.839 --> 01:00:30.295
Lint the mechanical failures before humans review (L1).

759
01:00:30.575 --> 01:00:39.300
Draw with C4: labeled, directional arrows; one abstraction level per diagram; mostly Levels 1 to 2 (§2.4).

760
01:00:40.650 --> 01:00:46.645
Generate diagrams from one model so they can't contradict each other, and validate the model (L2).

761
01:00:46.925 --> 01:00:51.981
Numbers come with assumptions and working so reviewers can check and challenge them (L3).

762
01:00:52.261 --> 01:00:57.829
Reviews are async-first, with named asks and a recorded outcome (§2.6).

763
01:00:58.109 --> 01:01:05.767
Keep architecture documentation true with docs-as-code, generation, fitness functions and drift detection (L4).

764
01:01:07.117 --> 01:01:08.440
Decision cheat sheet.

765
01:01:08.720 --> 01:01:10.064
Here is a quick table:

766
01:01:10.344 --> 01:01:12.840
Situation, and Artifact or practice.

767
01:01:13.120 --> 01:01:14.763
Should we invest in this at all?:

768
01:01:15.043 --> 01:01:16.003
One-pager.

769
01:01:16.283 --> 01:01:18.629
Building something non-trivial or cross-team:

770
01:01:18.909 --> 01:01:21.597
Design doc (§2.3 template).

771
01:01:21.877 --> 01:01:23.435
One significant decision:

772
01:01:23.715 --> 01:01:25.293
ADR (Ch 31).

773
01:01:25.573 --> 01:01:28.219
Change affecting many teams or a new standard:

774
01:01:28.499 --> 01:01:30.376
RFC with a named decider.

775
01:01:30.656 --> 01:01:34.901
Documenting an existing system: arc42 + C4 model.

776
01:01:35.181 --> 01:01:36.717
Diagrams for a design:

777
01:01:36.997 --> 01:01:42.907
C4 L1 + L2, sequence diagrams for key flows, generated from a model.

778
01:01:44.257 --> 01:01:45.451
Capacity figures:

779
01:01:45.731 --> 01:01:49.230
Assumptions, then derivations, recomputable (L3).

780
01:01:49.510 --> 01:01:50.769
Keeping docs true:

781
01:01:51.049 --> 01:01:56.062
Docs-as-code, generated references, drift detection (L4), fitness functions.

782
01:01:56.342 --> 01:01:57.622
Getting a good review:

783
01:01:57.902 --> 01:02:01.230
Lint first, named asks, async window, recorded outcome.

784
01:02:02.580 --> 01:02:03.711
Exercises.

785
01:02:03.991 --> 01:02:15.276
(B2) Take a design doc from your work (or §1.1's description) and rewrite its first page using the template's Summary, Context, Goals, Non-goals and Requirements.

786
01:02:15.556 --> 01:02:17.476
Run L1 on before and after.

787
01:02:17.756 --> 01:02:22.599
(B2) Draw the C4 context and container diagrams for your current system.

788
01:02:22.879 --> 01:02:25.332
Label every arrow with verb and protocol.

789
01:02:25.612 --> 01:02:26.807
What did you discover?

790
01:02:27.087 --> 01:02:33.551
(B3) Extend L2 with a deployment view: map containers to Kubernetes namespaces and nodes.

791
01:02:33.831 --> 01:02:36.924
Add a validation that every container has a deployment.

792
01:02:38.274 --> 01:02:46.765
(B3) Extend L3 to read assumptions and claims from a YAML block in a real Markdown doc, so it can run as a CI check.

793
01:02:47.045 --> 01:02:56.559
(B4) Extend L4 to consume a dependency list exported from distributed traces (a CSV of caller, then callee) in addition to code.

794
01:02:56.839 --> 01:02:59.826
Which dependencies appear only in traces, and why?

795
